ildings at grade 4+",poorWeighted:"Used floor area at grade 4+",gradeMix:"Condition mix",grade:"Grade",gradeGood:"Grade 1 — sound",gradeBad:"Grade 5 — unusable",curve:"Backlog over time",crossover:"The two measures disagree: spreading the money renovates more buildings, prioritising protects more of the floor area people are actually in. That is why this is a decision rather than a calculation.",provenance:"Seed {{seed}} · annual budget {{budget}} · every figure comes from tools/geodata/build_condition.py"},nM={provenanceTitle:"Real routes, estimated head counts",$provenanceComment:"⚠️ ONLY GARCHING OFFERS THIS LENS. What stood here described a generated timetable — which is OTH and LMU, neither of which has the lens at all. Garching's transitions come from real TUMonline bookings; only the head counts are synthetic.",provenanceBody:"The room changes and timings come from real TUMonline bookings, and the routes from the real OpenStreetMap footpath network. ⚠️ How many people are walking is synthetic: derived seats times an assumed fill factor. Stairs are weighted ×2.2 — a model of effort, not a measurement.",transitions:"Routed transitions",courses:"Courses",peak:"Peak",when:"Time",timeline:"The teaching week — click or drag to pick a time",prevPeak:"Previous peak",nextPeak:"Next peak",peakCount:"{{count}} peaks",wholeWeek:"Whole week",showSlot:"Show the peak",walking:"walking",note:"Each ribbon is a path segment and its width is the load. The animation shows direction and density — it is not a simulation of individual people."},iM={heading:"Places"},aM={idle:"Press W A S D to fly",hint:"W A S D to fly · Q / E altitude · R / F circle the view centre · drag or arrow keys to look · wheel for cruise speed · Shift to boost · Esc to leave",altitude:"Altitude AMSL",agl:"Above ground",speed:"Speed",heading:"Heading",cruise:"Cruise",offMap:"off the map"},sM={more:"Further sources and licences: NOTICE.md"},rM={label:"Source",pending:"source missing"},oM={reconstruction:"reconstruction"},lM={aachen:{audimax:"RWTH Aachen — at some 45,000 students, Germany's largest technical university.",templergraben:"The historic core on Templergraben: main building, library, mechanical engineering.",westcampus:"Two and a half kilometres separate the two halves — and they have their own station.",melaten:"Campus Melaten: the research estate that grew after the historic core ran out of room."},fau:{schloss:"The palace is the university: a margravial residence of 1704, FAU's main building since 1825.",technische:"The Faculty of Engineering on the Südgelände — the part of FAU that looks like a campus.",informatik:"Computer science and the computing centre: Erlangen has worked here since the early days of computer architecture.",roethelheim:"Röthelheim campus — a former barracks that became a university."},garching:{campus:"Garching research campus — some 60 buildings on the Munich gravel plain.",ubahn:"The U6 terminates in the middle of the campus. Its timetable decides when it gets crowded.",mw:"Mechanical Engineering: eight connected wings, around 2,700 mapped rooms.",mi:"Mathematics and Informatics — home to the most heavily booked lecture hall.",frm2:"The research reactor defines the campus skyline."},koeln:{campus:"The University of Cologne — at some 47,000 students, the largest university in this model.",hoersaal:"The lecture hall building on Albertus-Magnus-Platz: where the crowd arrives.",philosophikum:"Philosophikum — a single building for one of Germany's largest faculties.",biozentrum:"The Biozentrum and the institutes along Zülpicher Straße."},muenster:{schloss:"The university's main building is a baroque palace — the rectorate has sat here since 1954.",hoersaal:"The lecture hall building on Schlossplatz: the working day behind the baroque facade.",bibliothek:"The university library, which serves the region as well as the university.",naturwissenschaften:"The sciences sit two kilometres west, at the Coesfelder Kreuz."},lmu:{hauptgebaeude:"The main building on Geschwister-Scholl-Platz — the yardstick everything here is measured against.",philologicum:"The Philologicum on Ludwigstraße: much of the teaching stock sits along this axis.",informatik:"Institute for Computer Science, Oettingenstraße 67 — the one building with a real interior: 707 rooms on three levels, their outlines read off LMU's own published floor plans.",klinikum:"University Hospital city campus — 2.4 km south. That is an U-Bahn ride, not a fifteen-minute break.",anatomie:"The Anatomische Anstalt on Pettenkoferstraße: pre-clinical teaching here, the lecture on the main campus — exactly the conflict this is about."},oth:{campus:"Seybothstraße campus on the Galgenberg — the larger of the two sites.",hoersaal:"The lecture-hall building: the large events everything else is planned around.",informatik:"Faculty of Computer Science and Mathematics on Galgenbergstraße.",pruefening:"Prüfeninger Straße campus — 2.5 km away. That distance is what the plan has to work around."},tuebingen:{schloss:"Hohentübingen Castle above the old town — institutes inside a fortress.",neckarfront:"The Neckar front and the Platanenallee: the image Tübingen is known for.",altstadt:"The university is not a compound; it is threaded through a medieval town.",aula:"Neue Aula — the university's ceremonial centre.",kupferbau:"Kupferbau: the large post-war lecture theatres."}},cM={title:"Guided tour",open:"Guide",close:"Close the tour",back:"Back",next:"Next",done:"Done",$sourceComment:"⚠️ The badge on each card is the point of the tour. This dataset mixes surveyed geography, published floor plans and an invented timetable, and a viewer cannot tell them apart by looking.",source:{overview:"Overview",survey:"Official survey",published:"Published floor plans",generated:"Generated timetable",solver:"CP-SAT optimisation",foundry:"Azure AI Foundry",fabric:"Fabric SQL"},step:{welcome:{title:"The Friday that moves everything",body:"Timetabling at {{university}} is still largely manual today: availabilities arrive as Excel files, the plan is built by hand — and when a lecturer drops out, somebody has to work out what that knocks over. This tour walks through exactly that case, from the cancellation to a confirmed new plan. At every step it says where the data came from."},welcomeTwin:{title:"A campus you can take hold of",body:"This is a digital twin of {{university}} — terrain, buildings and vegetation from official data. No timetable exists for this site, so the app shows no planning here and claims none. This tour shows what is measured and what is not."},campus:{title:"The map is not an illustration",body:"The ground comes from the official 2 m elevation model, the buildings from the state survey's LoD2 data, the aerial image and the trees from open cadastral data. None of it is drawn by hand — which is why distances, heights and walking routes agree with reality. You are seeing this university's sites in their true positions relative to each other."},distance:{title:"Two sites, one fifteen-minute break",body:"Below are the journeys this plan asks people to make — computed on the real footpath network, and by bus between the sites. The break between two blocks is 15 minutes: if that is not enough for the journey, the change counts as a conflict. Between two sites a single minute often decides it — which is why travel time is a hard rule here rather than a nice-to-have."},scenario:{title:"The cancellation arrives",body:"Now the everyday case: a lecturer can no longer teach on Fridays. Today this is where the handwork starts — find the affected sessions, find replacement slots, check what breaks next. ▶ Click the first example question below. It already names the lecturer who carries the most teaching in this plan."},cascade:{title:"What hangs off it?",body:"This is the step that costs an afternoon today: which sessions fall away, which student groups are left stranded, which rooms free up, who would have to step in? The assistant reads that out of the plan instead of estimating it, and the affected sessions sit in the week above. One cancellation becomes its full consequences in seconds."},review:{title:"A proposal, not a decision",body:"Several options come back, each with the number of sessions it moves. The proposal is laid into the week as a preview first — you see the change before it counts. A human decides whether to apply or discard it. This is precisely the review-and-adjust surface the target picture asks for: the machine computes, the planner is accountable."},studentDay:{title:"The day the students actually live",body:"Idle hours, day length, unpopular fringe slots — and campus changes where the break is too short for the journey. These are the soft requirements from the target picture that a pure conflict check never catches: it is satisfied the moment nothing collides. This says what the plan costs the people in it, which is what makes it improvable."},calendar:{title:"The week the planning happens in",body:"The grid shows a teaching week — by room, by student group or by lecturer. Sessions can be dragged, and the plan judges the move immediately: room free, capacity sufficient, lecturer available, student group not double-booked. ⚠️ The student groups, lecturers and sessions are generated rather than observed: the dataset is plausible, but it is nobody's actual timetable."},interior:{title:"Real rooms wherever real plans exist",body:"Clicking a building opens it up by storey. Where the university publishes floor plans, the room outlines are read off those very drawings. Every other floor plate is generated, and the dataset marks it as such. Capacity and room type hang off each individual room, because without them any replanning is guesswork."},lenses:{title:"Conflict-free is not the same as good",body:"A plan can satisfy every hard rule and still be miserable to live with. So here is the same week from three points of view: rooms, lecturers, students — how hard the lecture halls are working, who is over their contracted teaching load, and what a student group's day actually looks like."},assistant:{title:"Ask in prose, get an answer with its working",body:"The assistant is an agent in Azure AI Foundry. It answers nothing from memory: it calls tools against the dataset and shows which ones — the tool trace under the answer is the evidence. No jargon and no fixed phrasing is required. ⚠️ The language model never places a session itself."},solver:{title:"Repair means changing as little as possible",body:"A CP-SAT optimiser computes the repair — provably optimal, and explicitly aiming to move as few sessions as it can. It knows the same rules the checker does: availabilities, room capacity, double-booking — and the travel time between the sites. A proposal that sends somebody across the city in a fifteen-minute break is never offered in the first place."},saving:{title:"A confirmed change outlives the container",body:"Every confirmed change is written to Fabric SQL — once as a log entry, and once as the session's current position. The plan is restored from that the next time the drawer opens, because the compute scales to zero and forgets everything it held. The change list stays auditable: who moved what, and when."},provenance:{title:"What is real here, and what is not",body:"Measured: the terrain, the buildings, the trees, the footpaths — and the room outlines wherever the university publishes plans. Invented: the students, the lecturers, the courses and the whole timetable. In production the Untis export and the lecturers' own availabilities would take their place. The app says so at every figure, because a confident wrong answer is worse than an open gap."},realData:{title:"This timetable is real",body:"The week on screen comes from TUMonline: 1,470 published bookings in 122 rooms, plus 1,699 hours where a room is occupied by something that is not teaching. The other universities publish no timetable, so theirs is generated. Only the lecturers and the student groups are invented here — TUMonline names neither, and the app refuses to answer questions about a person."}}},uM={heading:"Planning assistant",intro:"Ask in ordinary sentences — no jargon, no fixed phrasing needed. The assistant works the answer out and shows each step, so where a number came from stays traceable. It replies in German.",placeholder:"e.g. Prof. Meier can no longer teach on Fridays …",send:"Ask",clear:"Clear chat",thinking:"thinking and solving …",notConfigured:"The planning service is not set up (VITE_SCHEDULER_API). The 3D view still works, the assistant does not.",errAgent:"The assistant could not finish this request.",errRequest:"The assistant is unreachable right now. Please try again."},dM={hide:"Hide side panel",show:"Show side panel"},hM={loading:"Loading the campus",loadingStart:"Connecting …",loadingStep:"Step {{step}} of {{of}}",loading_terrain:"Terrain model and surroundings",loading_drape:"Aerial photograph, 20 cm resolution",loading_buildings:"LoD2 buildings from the cadastre",loading_vegetation:"Trees from the surface model",errScene:"The 3D view could not be loaded. Please reload the page."},fM={switch:"Choose a university",noPlanner:"No timetable has been loaded for this location. Buildings, rooms and the lenses are here; the calendar and the assistant are not."},mM={toLight:"Switch to the light view",toDark:"Switch to the dark view"},pM={hint:"Drag to move this session to another time",frozen:"This session is fixed and cannot be moved",target:"{{from}} → {{to}}",room:"Room",discard:"Discard",checking:"Checking …",checkFailed:"The check failed — this move has NOT been judged.",legal:"No conflicts — this slot works.",notSaved:"What-if: this move is not saved. Only a fully worked-out proposal can become a draft, and only once you have confirmed it.",clashRoom:"Room {{room}} is already booked at that time",clashTeacher:"{{teacher}} is already teaching at that time",clashCohort:"Student group {{cohort}} already has a lectur